---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { calculateSalary } from '../../lib/engine';
import { MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY, LIVING_WAGE_HOURLY } from '../../lib/tax-rates-2026';

// Les montants nets viennent du moteur du site, jamais d'un calcul a part :
// une page qui annoncerait un net different de celui que le calculateur
// affiche detruirait la confiance qu'elle cherche a etablir. Constate le
// 2026-09-26 sur l'accueil, qui affichait un net de 24 700 EUR la ou le
// moteur en donnait 24 158.
const SEMAINE = 39;
const brut = (taux: number) => Math.round(taux * SEMAINE * 52 * 100) / 100;
const net = (taux: number) =>
  calculateSalary({ grossAnnual: brut(taux), filingStatus: 'single', pensionRate: 0, additionalCredits: 0 });

const minimum = net(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY);
const living = net(LIVING_WAGE_HOURLY);
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Can an employer pay less than the national minimum wage?',
    answer: 'Only in the narrow cases the Act allows: close relatives of the employer, people on a statutory apprenticeship, and those under twenty, who have their own sub-minimum rates. Outside those cases the rate is a floor that no contract, collective agreement or consent can lower, and an agreement to accept less has no legal effect.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do tips count towards the minimum wage?',
    answer: 'No. Since the Payment of Wages Amendment Act 2022, tips and gratuities cannot be used to make up the minimum wage, and they must be distributed fairly and transparently. Service charges presented to customers as going to staff must be paid to staff. An employer counting tips towards the hourly floor is in breach on both counts.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I have been underpaid?',
    answer: 'A complaint can be made to the Workplace Relations Commission within six months of the underpayment, extendable to twelve where there is reasonable cause. The adjudication can award arrears, and the Act makes penalisation for having made a complaint a separate offence. Records must be kept by the employer for three years, and their absence counts against them.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does the minimum wage apply to part-time work?',
    answer: 'Yes, at exactly the same hourly rate. The rate is hourly, so it applies irrespective of the number of hours worked, and there is no threshold below which it ceases to apply. What changes with part-time work is only the weekly and annual total, along with the point at which PRSI and USC thresholds are crossed.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does it apply during a trial or probation period?',
    answer: 'Yes. There is no probationary exemption in Irish law, and an unpaid trial shift is unlawful where work of value is performed. Training required by the employer counts as working time and must be paid. The only sub-minimum rates that exist are age-based and apply to employees under twenty, regardless of probation.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the living wage a legal obligation?',
    answer: 'Not yet. The living wage is a target figure, calculated on the cost of a minimum essential standard of living, towards which the statutory minimum is being raised in steps. Until the transition is complete, only the national minimum wage is legally enforceable, and the living wage figure serves as a benchmark rather than a right.',
  },
];

const eur = (n: number) => Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-IE');
const eur2 = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-IE', { minimumFractionDigits: 2, maximumFractionDigits: 2 });

// Les taux reduits sont exprimes en pourcentage du taux plein par la
// legislation, pas en montants fixes : ils suivent donc automatiquement.
// Le taux en vigueur au premier semestre de chaque annee, publie par Eurostat
// en montant mensuel et ramene a l'heure par la formule officielle
// (mensuel x 12 / 52 / 39). La serie reproduit exactement les taux connus,
// ce qui valide la conversion.
const historique = [
  { an: 2019, taux: 9.80 }, { an: 2020, taux: 9.80 }, { an: 2021, taux: 10.20 },
  { an: 2022, taux: 10.50 }, { an: 2023, taux: 11.30 }, { an: 2024, taux: 12.70 },
  { an: 2025, taux: 13.50 }, { an: 2026, taux: 14.15 },
];

const reduits = [
  { age: 'Aged 20 and over', part: 1.00, libelle: 'Full rate' },
  { age: 'Aged 19', part: 0.90, libelle: '90 % of the full rate' },
  { age: 'Aged 18', part: 0.80, libelle: '80 % of the full rate' },
  { age: 'Under 18', part: 0.70, libelle: '70 % of the full rate' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Minimum Wage Ireland 2026: Rates, Take-Home Pay and Rules"
  description="The national minimum wage is €14.15 an hour in 2026. What it leaves after tax, the reduced rates for young workers, and how it sits against the living wage."
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-6">
    <ol class="flex items-center gap-1">
      <li><a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a></li>
      <li class="before:content-['/'] before:mx-1">Minimum Wage Ireland</li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Minimum Wage Ireland 2026: Rates, Take-Home Pay and Rules</h1>

    <p>
      The national minimum wage in Ireland is <strong>&euro;{eur2(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY)} an hour</strong>
      in 2026, for employees aged twenty and over. On a thirty-nine hour week that is
      &euro;{eur(brut(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY))} a year before deductions, and
      <strong>&euro;{eur(minimum.netAnnual)}</strong> after income tax, USC and PRSI for a single
      employee with the standard credits, which works out at about
      &euro;{eur(minimum.netMonthly)} a month. Those net figures come from the same calculation engine
      that powers the calculator on this site, so the numbers here and the numbers it produces agree.
      The minimum wage is a legal floor, not a recommendation: paying less is an offence, and the
      obligation applies whatever the contract says. It is set as an hourly rate, which matters more
      than it sounds, because entitlement is calculated over a pay reference period rather than per
      shift. What follows sets out the reduced rates for younger workers, what actually counts as
      pay for the purpose of meeting the floor, and how the minimum compares with the living wage.
    </p>

    <h2>The rates in 2026</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Age</th><th>Share of full rate</th><th>Hourly</th><th>Annual equivalent</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {reduits.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.age}</td>
            <td>{r.libelle}</td>
            <td>&euro;{eur2(Math.round(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY * r.part * 100) / 100)}</td>
            <td>&euro;{eur(brut(Math.round(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY * r.part * 100) / 100))}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The reduced rates are set in law as percentages of the full rate rather than as fixed amounts,
      so they move automatically whenever the headline rate changes. Annual equivalents assume a
      thirty-nine hour week across fifty-two weeks and are our own arithmetic, not an official figure.
    </p>

    <h2>What the minimum wage leaves after deductions</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>Minimum wage</th><th>Living wage</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hourly</td><td>&euro;{eur2(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY)}</td><td>&euro;{eur2(LIVING_WAGE_HOURLY)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gross annual, 39 hours</td><td>&euro;{eur(brut(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY))}</td><td>&euro;{eur(brut(LIVING_WAGE_HOURLY))}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Net annual</td><td>&euro;{eur(minimum.netAnnual)}</td><td>&euro;{eur(living.netAnnual)}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Net monthly</td><td>&euro;{eur(minimum.netMonthly)}</td><td>&euro;{eur(living.netMonthly)}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The difference between the two is &euro;{eur(brut(LIVING_WAGE_HOURLY) - brut(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY))}
      a year gross, but only
      &euro;{eur(living.netAnnual - minimum.netAnnual)} after deductions. Roughly a quarter of the
      gross gap disappears into tax, USC and PRSI, which is worth knowing before treating the two
      hourly rates as directly comparable measures of living standards.
    </p>
    <p>
      At this level of income the income tax bill is small, because tax credits reduce it directly
      rather than reducing taxable income. USC and PRSI still apply and account for most of what is
      deducted. Someone on the minimum wage for a full year stays well below the standard rate
      cut-off point, so none of that income is taxed at the higher rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      These figures assume a single employee with the standard credits and no pension contribution.
      Change any of those and the net changes: the <a href="/">calculator</a> takes filing status,
      pension rate and additional credits, and applies the same engine used here.
    </p>

    <h2>How the rate has moved since 2019</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Year</th><th>Hourly rate</th><th>Change</th><th>Gross annual, 39 hours</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {historique.map((r, i) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.an}</td>
            <td>&euro;{eur2(r.taux)}</td>
            <td>{i === 0 ? ', ' : (r.taux === historique[i - 1].taux ? 'unchanged' : `+${(100 * (r.taux - historique[i - 1].taux) / historique[i - 1].taux).toFixed(1)} %`)}</td>
            <td>&euro;{eur(brut(r.taux))}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The rate rose from &euro;{eur2(9.80)} in 2019 to &euro;{eur2(MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY)} in 2026, an
      increase of {(100 * (MINIMUM_WAGE_HOURLY - 9.80) / 9.80).toFixed(0)} per cent across seven
      years. The three largest rises fall in 2023, 2024 and 2025, reflecting a period of high
      consumer price inflation and a stated policy of moving the floor towards the living wage.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the nominal rate, not what it buys. A rise that trails inflation is a cut in real
      terms, and comparing this series with consumer prices is the only way to tell which years were
      which. That comparison needs a different dataset and is outside what this page covers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rates shown are those in force at the start of each year. Increases usually take effect on
      1 January following the Budget, though that has not always been the case, so a rate that
      changed mid-year may appear here under the year it began rather than the year it was announced.
    </p>

    <h2>What counts as pay towards the minimum</h2>

    <p>
      This is where disputes usually start, because not everything an employer pays counts towards
      the floor.
    </p>
    <p>
      Basic pay counts, as do shift premiums, productivity bonuses and, within limits set in
      regulation, board and lodging provided by the employer. Overtime premiums, tips, expenses,
      allowances for special duties and public holiday premium payments do not count towards the
      minimum, even though they are real money in the employee's pocket.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical consequence is that an employee whose total pay looks comfortably above the floor
      can still be underpaid once the non-reckonable elements are stripped out. Anyone checking a
      payslip should therefore compare the reckonable pay against the hours worked in the pay
      reference period, not the gross total against the hourly rate.
    </p>

    <h2>The pay reference period</h2>

    <p>
      Entitlement is not assessed shift by shift. The employer sets a pay reference period, which
      cannot exceed one month, and the test is whether average reckonable pay across that period
      meets the hourly minimum.
    </p>
    <p>
      That has a consequence people find counter-intuitive: a single week paid below the rate is not
      automatically a breach if the average across the reference period clears it. Conversely,
      averaging cannot be used to justify a persistent shortfall.
    </p>
    <p>
      An employee is entitled to ask their employer in writing for a statement of average hourly pay
      for a given reference period, and the employer must supply it. That statement is the starting
      point for any complaint, and it is worth requesting before escalating anywhere else.
    </p>

    <h2>Where these figures come from</h2>

    <p>
      The hourly rate and the reduced percentages are statutory. The gross annual equivalents are our
      own arithmetic, the hourly rate multiplied by thirty-nine hours and fifty-two weeks, and are
      labelled as such wherever they appear.
    </p>
    <p>
      The net amounts are produced by this site's own calculation engine, applying the 2026 income
      tax bands, tax credits, USC rates and PRSI. Our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>
      sets out exactly which rates are used and where each comes from.
    </p>
    <p>
      Statutory rates change, typically at the start of a calendar year following the Budget. If you
      are reading this well into a later year, check the current rate before relying on it: a page
      that quietly keeps last year's figure is worse than no page, and that is precisely the mistake
      this one was written to correct.
    </p>

    <h2>The sub-minimum rates for employees under twenty</h2>

    <p>
      Irish law still permits lower statutory rates for younger employees, set as percentages of the
      full national minimum wage: those aged under eighteen may be paid seventy per cent of it, those
      aged eighteen ninety per cent, and those aged nineteen ninety per cent as well under the
      structure introduced in 2019. From the twentieth birthday the full rate applies, and the
      employer is expected to move to it in the pay period in which the birthday falls rather than at
      the end of the year. These are ceilings on what may lawfully be deducted from the adult rate,
      not target rates: nothing prevents an employer from paying the full rate to a seventeen-year-old,
      and many collective agreements require it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two points about the sub-minimum rates are frequently misunderstood. They are age-based rather
      than experience-based, so a nineteen-year-old with three years of service is still legally
      payable at the reduced rate while a twenty-year-old on their first day is not. And the training
      rates that used to allow reduced pay during structured training were abolished in 2019, so an
      employer citing a training rate for an adult employee is relying on a provision that no longer
      exists. The Low Pay Commission has recommended the phasing out of the remaining age-based rates,
      and any change would come through an amending order rather than through negotiation.
    </p>

    <h2>Deductions an employer may and may not make</h2>

    <p>
      The minimum wage is a floor on gross pay, and the Payment of Wages Act governs what may come off
      it. Statutory deductions, income tax, USC and PRSI, are always permitted. A deduction required
      or authorised by the contract, such as a pension contribution the employee has agreed to, is
      permitted if the contract was given in writing in advance. A deduction for something provided by
      the employer, such as accommodation or a uniform, is permitted only within limits the Act sets,
      and only where it is fair and reasonable in the circumstances.
    </p>

    <p>
      What may not come off is anything that has the effect of taking pay below the statutory minimum
      for time actually worked. Till shortages, breakages, customer walk-outs and the cost of required
      uniforms cannot be recovered from wages where doing so would breach the floor, and a deduction
      for an over-payment must be agreed in writing and spread so as not to breach it either.
      Employers must keep records of hours and pay for three years, and in a Workplace Relations
      Commission complaint the absence of those records is taken against the employer rather than the
      employee.
    </p>

    <h2>Who enforces the rate, and how a complaint proceeds</h2>

    <p>
      Enforcement sits with the Workplace Relations Commission, whose inspectors may enter a workplace,
      examine records and interview employees without notice. An individual complaint is made on a
      standard form within six months of the contravention, and an adjudication officer holds a hearing
      at which both sides may be represented. The remedy is payment of the arrears, and the officer may
      also order compensation where the breach was accompanied by penalisation. Appeals go to the
      Labour Court within forty-two days, and a determination may be enforced through the District
      Court. Nothing in the process requires the employment to have ended, and dismissal for having
      made a complaint is itself actionable.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two practical notes on evidence. Keep a personal record of hours worked, since the statutory
      three-year record-keeping duty falls on the employer and a dispute about hours is far easier to
      win with a contemporaneous note than without one. And ask for the written statement of terms that
      the Terms of Employment Information Act requires within five days of starting: it states the
      hourly rate, and its absence is itself a separate contravention.
    </p>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>

    {faqs.map((f) => (
      <div>
        <h3>{f.question}</h3>
        <p>{f.answer}</p>
      </div>
    ))}

    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.question,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.answer },
      })),
    })} />

  </div>
</Layout>
